.TH WSLG-FIX.SERVICE 8 "%DATE%" "%NAME% %VERSION%"
.SH NAME
wslg\-fix.service \- ensure write access to X11 socket for WSLg
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B wslg-fix.service
.SH DESCRIPTION
The X Window System, Version 11 (X11) makes use of UNIX domain sockets
(see \fIunix\fP(7)) whenever a client application is to run on the same machine
as its display server. These sockets are accessible as files stored in the
directory \fI/tmp/.X11-unix\fP. Under the Windows Subsystem for Linux GUI
(WSLg), this directory is set as read-only by default, which prevents more
sophisticated applications, such as desktop environments, from working.
\fIwslg-fix.service\fP automatically recreates \fI/tmp/.X11-unix\fP upon startup, sets
the correct access permissions for it and the Wayland lock file, and links
the sockets to those used by WSLg.
.PP
If you have installed @NAME@ from your package manager on a systemd-based
operating system, \fIwslg-fix.service\fP should be enabled by default. If this
is not the case, and your operating system uses systemd, run 'systemctl
\fBenable\fP wslg-fix.service'.
.SH "SEE ALSO"
\fIwsl\-tools\-services\fP(8)
